abstract: >-
  An aggregated database of 185 sources and 2404 numeric indicators on public
  attitudes to radical life extension, immortality, longevity and healthy
  living. It covers measurements for 109 countries and cross-national studies
  of up to 183 countries; attitude measurements span 1973–2026, while
  demographic series begin in 1751.
  Key finding: the share who agree falls sevenfold as the answer grows more
  costly — from 69.7 % for the value judgement “living long is a good thing”
  to 9.7 % for measured behaviour. The data come with a showcase of 37 charts
  and a coverage matrix that makes the geographic and thematic blanks visible.
